      "rationale": "A boolean flag for perceived cutbacks could add some leverage on decline complaints, but it is heavily skewed (833 false) and somewhat generic; its usefulness overlaps conceptually with perceived_service_decline. Sampled values are all plausibly grounded (false where no such signal)."
    },
    {
      "name": "cabin_class_value_gap",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.9,
      "source_groundedness": 0.85,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Flagging whether a class underdelivered relative to expectation is a valuable axis for value-for-money analysis and non-recommendation drivers. Mostly grounded, though 'economy_as_expected' is sometimes assigned to reviews that don't explicitly state class (e.g. Phuket seats review), a mild inference risk but generally defensible."
    },
    {
      "name": "perceived_service_decline",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.7,
      "source_groundedness": 0.9,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Capturing whether a passenger perceives the airline declining is a meaningful signal for a premium carrier's reputation and recommendation. Somewhat overlaps with amenity_reduction_signal but adds a distinct sentiment-over-time angle. Sampled values grounded (false where no decline mentioned)."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.85,
  "source_groundedness": 0.89,
  "rationale": "The added columns provide several genuinely useful analytical axes (crew service quality, food issue typing, cabin-class value gap, perceived decline) that directly support diagnosing why passengers do not recommend Singapore Airlines and where to improve. Two boolean signals partially overlap but each still adds a distinct angle. Groundedness is high overall: silent rows are sensibly marked Unknown/false, and most produced values are sound readings of source text. The main weakness is occasional over-inference, notably labelling a crew-silent seat-comfort review as 'inattentive_or_rude', which slightly lowers groundedness."
